<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2"><Document><name>ILLUSTRATIVE shenzhou-24 ascent</name><description>CONFIRMED 2026-11-24, no utc shift: T0 2026-11-25T00:18:00Z is the CMSA targeted instant (Beijing 08:18 on 25 Nov), and it already matched the files. Xinhua (https://www.news.cn/20261124/18101736be3f4ef098c062ec82fb2a2a/c.html) and CASC, citing CMSA (https://www.spacechina.com/n25/n2014789/n2014804/c4764137/content.html): crew Tang Hongbo (commander; spacecraft pilot), Wu Yi (flight engineer), Muhammad Zeeshan Ali (Pakistan; payload specialist). After insertion the plan is a 3.5-hour fast autonomous rendezvous, docking at the Tianhe forward port. Instantaneous: the LL2 window 00:08-00:48Z is not the clock used here. LL2 id c9605b67-8a53-4239-a8ae-577fe3b79799 re-queried this run: still Go, net 2026-11-25T00:18:00Z, minute precision, last_updated 2026-11-20T09:07:56Z, no result. ECEF unchanged. ILLUSTRATIVE.
ILLUSTRATIVE ASCENT ECEF — Long March 2F/G Shenzhou-24 (Jiuquan LA-91 / SLS-1) — NOT OFFICIAL TELEMETRY
PRE-FLIGHT. Not telemetry. T0 is the LL2 NET 2026-11-25T00:18:00Z (Tue 24 Nov 4:18 p.m. PST; Wed 25 Nov 08:18 Beijing), status Go, window 00:08-00:48Z, minute precision, last_updated 2026-11-20T09:07:56Z.
https://ll.thespacedevs.com/2.3.0/launches/c9605b67-8a53-4239-a8ae-577fe3b79799/
TIME CONFLICT, not silent. CMSA and Xinhua (21 Nov 2026, English and Chinese) give no liftoff clock. CMSA&#x27;s flight list still ends at Shenzhou-23. Wikipedia EN lists 00:19 UTC and cites Next Spaceflight; Next Spaceflight lists 00:18 UTC. 00:19 was not used. 05:18 AM PKT equals 00:18Z (PKT is UTC+5); no Pakistani post stating 05:18 was retrieved, so that wording is not an independent source.
Surrogate: event knots on a constant-azimuth spherical rhumb (R=6371.0088 km) at 97.16 deg. Knot times are Baidu encyclopedia&#x27;s theoretical crewed Long March 2F sequence, not Shenzhou-24&#x27;s own: booster sep 154.8 s, stage sep 159.5 s, fairing 212.5 s, S2 main cutoff 463.1 s. FILE ENDS AT S2 MAIN CUTOFF. The vernier tail and ship separation (theoretical 582.1 s / 585.1 s) are in the sim file.
Knot altitudes and ground ranges are THIS BOARD&#x27;S 3DOF SIM at those times, not published altitudes. The one geometric assumption is that those ranges lie on this flight&#x27;s constant-azimuth rhumb. Wikipedia&#x27;s Shenzhou profile says the escape tower comes off at about 40 km (also given as 39 km) at about T+120 s. The sim is at 32.7 km then. That 40 km figure was not substituted into the knots.
Azimuth 97.16 deg is the east-southeast supplement for Tiangong&#x27;s published 41.47 deg inclination from this pad: sin(az)=cos(41.47)/cos(40.957893). The 82.84 deg supplement was not used. No hazard polygon for 25 Nov 2026 00:08-00:48Z was found.
Conflicts recorded and not used as these knot times: English Wikipedia rounds the 2F/G booster burn to 153 s. CMSA Shenzhou-5 (basic rocket): boosters 137 s, stage sep 159 s, fairing 200 s, main cutoff 460 s, separation 587 s. CMSA Shenzhou-14: separation about 577 s. Wikipedia Shenzhou profile: boosters about 152 s, fairing about 205 s, main cutoff about 460 s, separation about 580 s.
Horizontal speed is d(range)/dt of the linear range between knots, plus the vertical rate, in local ENU. Not a published speed.
Pad (LL2): 40.957893N 100.290944E, elevation ASSUMED 0 m. Frame: WGS-84 ECEF, positions km, velocities km/s.
Constant-azimuth rhumb Az=97.16 deg from pad.
T0 used: 2026-11-25T00:18:00Z. Re-run with another T0 by shifting utc_iso only; ECEF geometry is Earth-fixed.</description>
